1. Nf3 d5 2. d4 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 c5 (4... c6) 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bg5 Be6 { [%cal Gg5f6] } 7. e3 (7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. e4 dxe4 9. Nxe4 Qf4 10. Bb5+ Nc6 11. Qe2 Be7 12. O-O) 7... Nc6?! { Inaccuracy. c4 was best. } (7... c4 8. Be2 Bb4 9. O-O Nbd7 10. Ne5 Qa5 11. Nxd7 Nxd7 12. Qc2) 8. Bb5 Be7 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. O-O O-O { [%cal Gg5f6,Gg7f6] } 11. Rc1?! { Inaccuracy. Bxf6 was best. } { missed a tactic to damage his structure or win the d5 pawn because of moving too quickly in the opening. Don't assume the opponent is playing the opening correctly. } (11. Bxf6 Qxf6 (11... gxf6 { same idea but with damaged structure } 12. Qa4 Ne5 13. Be2) 12. Nxd5 { wins a pawn } Qxb2 13. Nc7 Rac8 (13... Rad8 14. Qc1 Ba3 15. Qxb2 Bxb2 16. Rab1 Bf6 17. Nxe6 fxe6 18. Bc4 Rd6) 14. Nxe6 fxe6 15. Bc4 Qf6 16. Qd7 Rfe8 17. Bb3 Rcd8 (17... Na5 18. Bc2 Bxe3 19. fxe3 Rxc2 (19... Red8 20. Qa4 b5))) 11... Be7 12. Qa4 Qb6 13. Ne2 (13. Rfd1) 13... Rac8 { Stoyko position } { [%cal Ge2f4] } (13... h6) 14. Rfd1 { nf4 was best here. I was worried about bg4 but failed to notice the d5 pawn was hanging after bg4 } (14. Nf4 { [%cal Ge6g4] } a6 (14... h6 15. Bh4 g5 16. Nxe6) (14... Bg4) 15. Bxc6 Rxc6 16. Rxc6 bxc6 (16... Qxc6 17. Qxc6 bxc6 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Nd4 c5) 17. b3) (14. h3) 14... Ne4 15. Bxe7 Nxe7 16. Rxc8 (16. Nfd4 a6 (16... Nf5) 17. Bd3 (17. Qa3 Nf5 18. Bd3 Nxd4 19. Nxd4) 17... Qxb2 18. Rb1) 16... Rxc8 17. Bd7?? { Blunder. Nfd4 was best. } { huge blunder, because of nc5 forking - played after returning from toilet. Always blunder check properly before playing a move in classical, thankfully he didn't notice but I got lucky } (17. Nfd4 a6 18. Qa3 Qd6 19. Qxd6 Nxd6 20. Bd3 Nc6 21. b3 Nxd4 22. Nxd4 g5 23. f3 h5 24. Kf1 Kg7 25. Ke2 h4 26. Kd2 Kf6 27. Rh1 (27. Re1 a5 28. Rc1 Rxc1 29. Kxc1 Bf5 30. Kc2 Ke5 31. f4+ gxf4 32. Nf3+ Kf6 33. Nxh4 fxe3 34. Bxf5 Kg5 35. Bd3 Kxh4 36. Kc3 Kg5)) 17... Rc4?? { Blunder. Nc5 was best. } { [%cal Ga4a3,Ge6d7,Ga3e7] } (17... Nc5 18. Qh4 Ng6 19. Bxe6 Nxh4 20. Bxc8 Qxb2 21. Nfd4 g6 22. Rc1 (22. Nf4 Ne4 23. Nd3) 22... b6 23. a3) 18. Qb5?? { Blunder. Qa3 was best. } { 2 blunders in a row, probably frustrated with my previous error, always reset psychologically after a mistake and find the best move. qa3 was better, targetting the e7 knight. Apply the burger method, look for undefended pieces. } (18. Qa3 { qa3 attacks the e7 knight and defends b3. If the e7 knight is taken, the opponent runs into back rank problems. } Qb4 19. Bxe6 (19. Qxb4 Rxb4 20. Bxe6 fxe6 (20... Rxb2 21. Bxd5 Nxd5)) 19... Qxa3 20. Bxf7+ Kxf7 21. bxa3 Rc2 22. Ng3 Nxg3 23. hxg3 Rxa2) 18... Qxb5 19. Bxb5 Rc2?? { Blunder. Rb4 was best. } (19... Rb4 { rb4 forces the win of the b2 pawn. } 20. Nfd4 Rxb2 21. Rc1 Nf5 22. h3 Nxd4 23. Nxd4 Rxa2 24. Rc7 Ra1+ 25. Kh2) 20. Rb1 Nd2 21. Nfd4 (21. Nxd2 Rxd2 22. Nd4 Kf8 23. Kf1 Nf5 24. Ke1 Nxd4) 21... Rc7 22. Rd1 Ne4 23. f3 (23. Nf4 Bc8 (23... Bd7)) 23... Nd6 24. Bd3 h6 25. Kf2 a6 26. g4 Nc4 27. b3 Ne5 (27... Nb2) 28. Bb1 (28. Nf4) 28... N5c6 29. Rc1 (29. Nf5 { the most agressive move, recognizing the pawn is hard to target and well defended, and a thorn in black's kingside. } Bxf5 30. gxf5 { [%cal Ge2f4,Gh2h4,Gf4h5,Gf5f6] } Ne5 31. Nf4 Rc5 32. h4 N5c6 33. Nh5 Kf8 34. f6 gxf6 35. Nxf6 Kg7 36. Nd7 Rc3 37. Rg1+) 29... Rc8 30. h4 Nxd4 31. Rxc8+ Bxc8 32. Nxd4 Bd7 33. Bd3 Nc6 34. Nxc6 Bxc6 35. g5 hxg5 36. hxg5 Kf8 (36... a5) 37. Kg3 Ke7 38. Kf4 f6 { [%cal Gf4f5,Gf5g6] } 39. gxf6+ (39. Kf5 { better was to invade with the king. if fxg5 then black has 2 isolated pawn, if black doesn't do anything then the king is invading on g6 } fxg5 (39... Bd7+ 40. Kg6 Kf8 41. gxf6 Be8+ 42. Kf5) (39... d4) (39... Kf8) 40. Kxg5 d4 41. e4 (41. exd4 Bxf3 42. Kf4)) 39... Kxf6 (39... gxf6) (39... Ke8 40. fxg7) 40. e4 (40. a3 g5+ 41. Kg3) (40. b4 Bb5 (40... g5+ 41. Kg3 Ke5 42. Kg4 Bd7+ (42... Kf6 43. f4 Bd7+ 44. Kf3 Bc6 45. Kf2)) 41. Bxb5 axb5 42. Kg4 Kg6 43. a3 Kf6 44. f4 (44. e4)) (40. Kg4 Bd7+) 40... dxe4 (40... g5+) 41. Bxe4 Bxe4 42. Kxe4 g5 43. a3 a5 44. f4 *
